#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495279 :tutorial/content "A **unit price** is the price of a single item. To compute a unit price, we divide the total cost of a group of items by the number of items in the group.\n\nFor example, suppose a store sells a pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12.$ \n\nTo find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of shirts:\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3  = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[/math]4$ per shirt." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e8ab0cc-4a5e-57ca-a168-f89d110ff113" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3104 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495277 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that installing a fence costs $\\[math]360$ per yard. What would be the unit price per foot?\n\nFirst, notice that there are $3$ feet in $1$ yard.  \n\nTo find the unit price per foot, we *divide* the price per yard by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n360 \\div 3 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, installing the fence costs $\\[/math]120$ per foot.\n\nIn reverse, if we know that installing a fence costs $\\[math]120$ per foot, to find the unit price per yard, we would need to *multiply* the price per foot by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n120 \\times 3 = 360\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15c0e757-69bb-5bfd-a417-6de7c79fa333" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15094 :tutorial/title "Unit Prices With Units Conversions"}]}
